1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,920
If you're using PLS-SEM for your thesis,

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:05,396
there's a good chance you're making

3
00:00:05,426 --> 00:00:07,210
at least one of these seven mistakes.

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:08,761
And I'm not guessing.

5
00:00:08,791 --> 00:00:11,653
Every point in this video comes straight

6
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:15,818
from one of the most widely used
references on PLS-SEM:

7
00:00:15,848 --> 00:00:19,861
A Primer on Partial Least Squares
Structural Equation Modeling,

8
00:00:19,891 --> 00:00:23,252
third edition, by Hair, Hult, Ringle, and
Sarstedt.

9
00:00:23,282 --> 00:00:25,098
Let's get into it.

10
00:00:25,782 --> 00:00:27,112
Mistake number one:

11
00:00:27,142 --> 00:00:28,690
running a simultaneous F-test.

12
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:31,632
The F-test belongs to multiple regression.

13
00:00:31,662 --> 00:00:33,375
In chapter six, Hair lays out

14
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:34,937
how to evaluate a structural model:

15
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:37,835
check collinearity, test the path
coefficients,

16
00:00:37,865 --> 00:00:41,752
assess explanatory power, and assess
predictive power.

17
00:00:41,782 --> 00:00:44,131
There's no F-test on that list.

18
00:00:44,161 --> 00:00:46,171
If you want the combined effect of your
predictors,

19
00:00:46,201 --> 00:00:48,191
look at R squared.

20
00:00:48,221 --> 00:00:51,254
Hair describes it as the exogenous
variables'

21
00:00:51,284 --> 00:00:54,071
combined effects on the endogenous
variable.

22
00:00:54,101 --> 00:00:55,871
So instead of an F-test,

23
00:00:55,901 --> 00:01:00,259
report R squared together with the
significance of each path.

24
00:01:00,929 --> 00:01:02,139
Mistake number two:

25
00:01:02,169 --> 00:01:04,159
reporting a goodness-of-fit index.

26
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:06,599
The GoF was proposed early on

27
00:01:06,629 --> 00:01:10,439
as a way to validate PLS models globally.

28
00:01:10,469 --> 00:01:12,207
But research showed it can't separate

29
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:14,282
valid models from invalid ones.

30
00:01:14,312 --> 00:01:15,898
Hair's advice is direct:

31
00:01:15,928 --> 00:01:20,043
researchers are advised not to use this
measure in PLS-SEM.

32
00:01:20,073 --> 00:01:22,023
And the other proposed fit measures?

33
00:01:22,053 --> 00:01:23,543
In their current form,

34
00:01:23,573 --> 00:01:26,203
they offer little value for applied
research.

35
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:31,207
Focus on R squared, effect sizes, and
predictive power instead.

36
00:01:31,895 --> 00:01:33,145
Mistake number three:

37
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:36,585
choosing PLS-SEM just because your sample
is small.

38
00:01:36,615 --> 00:01:40,577
Hair calls this probably the most often
misused argument

39
00:01:40,607 --> 00:01:42,245
for using PLS-SEM.

40
00:01:42,275 --> 00:01:44,365
No method has magic capabilities.

41
00:01:44,395 --> 00:01:47,991
The popular ten-times rule is only a rough
guideline.

42
00:01:48,021 --> 00:01:50,731
Think in terms of statistical power
instead.

43
00:01:50,761 --> 00:01:52,631
With the inverse square root method,

44
00:01:52,661 --> 00:01:55,451
if your weakest expected path is about
0.2,

45
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:59,091
you need around 155 observations

46
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:01,211
at a 5% significance level.

47
00:02:01,241 --> 00:02:02,940
And if you ran a pilot study,

48
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:06,909
use its smallest path coefficient as your
starting point.

49
00:02:07,586 --> 00:02:08,896
Mistake number four:

50
00:02:08,926 --> 00:02:11,396
relying on the Fornell-Larcker criterion.

51
00:02:11,426 --> 00:02:13,721
For decades, it was the go-to test

52
00:02:13,751 --> 00:02:15,276
for discriminant validity.

53
00:02:15,306 --> 00:02:17,664
But Hair notes it performs very poorly

54
00:02:17,694 --> 00:02:19,996
when indicator loadings are similar,

55
00:02:20,026 --> 00:02:22,715
which is usually the case with real data.

56
00:02:22,745 --> 00:02:25,195
Use the HTMT instead:

57
00:02:25,225 --> 00:02:29,135
below 0.90, or below 0.85

58
00:02:29,165 --> 00:02:31,595
when your constructs are conceptually
distinct.

59
00:02:31,625 --> 00:02:35,335
In other words, an HTMT above 0.90

60
00:02:35,365 --> 00:02:38,247
is a red flag for discriminant validity.

61
00:02:38,966 --> 00:02:40,476
Mistake number five:

62
00:02:40,506 --> 00:02:42,816
testing mediation with the Sobel test.

63
00:02:42,846 --> 00:02:44,336
Hair is blunt here:

64
00:02:44,366 --> 00:02:46,916
the Sobel test should no longer be used.

65
00:02:46,946 --> 00:02:48,956
It assumes a normal distribution,

66
00:02:48,986 --> 00:02:52,976
which doesn't fit a nonparametric method
like PLS-SEM.

67
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:55,544
Bootstrap the indirect effect instead.

68
00:02:55,574 --> 00:02:58,664
Bootstrapping makes no distributional
assumptions,

69
00:02:58,694 --> 00:03:02,318
which is exactly why it suits PLS-SEM.

70
00:03:02,963 --> 00:03:04,793
Mistake number six:

71
00:03:04,823 --> 00:03:07,873
celebrating a reliability of 0.97.

72
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:11,953
Values between 0.70 and 0.90 are
satisfactory.

73
00:03:11,983 --> 00:03:14,853
But values above 0.90,

74
00:03:14,883 --> 00:03:18,733
and definitely above 0.95, are not
desirable.

75
00:03:18,763 --> 00:03:21,133
They usually mean your items are
redundant,

76
00:03:21,163 --> 00:03:24,203
slightly rephrasing the very same
question.

77
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:26,643
So if your reliability looks too perfect,

78
00:03:26,673 --> 00:03:30,214
check whether two items are asking the
same thing.

79
00:03:30,936 --> 00:03:32,526
Mistake number seven:

80
00:03:32,556 --> 00:03:35,446
judging R squared with one universal
cutoff.

81
00:03:35,476 --> 00:03:37,920
Hair says acceptable R squared values

82
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:39,526
are based on the context.

83
00:03:39,556 --> 00:03:42,801
In some disciplines, 0.10 is satisfactory.

84
00:03:42,831 --> 00:03:47,026
In others, researchers expect values above
0.65.

85
00:03:47,056 --> 00:03:47,797
Also remember:

86
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:51,865
the more predictors you add, the higher R
squared tends to get.

87
00:03:51,895 --> 00:03:54,665
So compare your R squared with similar
studies,

88
00:03:54,695 --> 00:03:56,890
not with a magic number.

89
00:03:57,496 --> 00:03:58,386
Quick recap.

90
00:03:58,416 --> 00:04:00,500
No F-test. No GoF.

91
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:02,286
No small-sample excuse.

92
00:04:02,316 --> 00:04:04,626
HTMT over Fornell-Larcker.

93
00:04:04,656 --> 00:04:06,366
Bootstrapping over Sobel.

94
00:04:06,396 --> 00:04:08,786
Watch out for reliability that's too high.

95
00:04:08,816 --> 00:04:10,786
And read R squared in context.

96
00:04:10,816 --> 00:04:13,706
If you want all of this in one practical
guide,

97
00:04:13,736 --> 00:04:17,457
check out the SEM-PLS guide in the
description.

98
00:04:17,487 --> 00:04:19,653
And subscribe to Not Your Lecture

99
00:04:19,683 --> 00:04:23,342
for more research tips that actually hold
up.

